  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
